INT. PET STORE, REPTILE SECTION - DAY

Heat lamps. Terrariums. A sign: PLEASE DO NOT KNOCK ON GLASS. The air smells of mealworms.

BARNABY "BARN" OKAFOR-LINDGREN (30s, apron, name tag crooked) restocks crickets. His coworker JUNIPER VASQUEZ-ABARA (20s, pink hair, bandaged thumb) leans on the counter. A customer, MR. EZEKIEL PRUITT-HALE (70s, tweed cap, binocular case), peers into a tank. At the back door, the manager, DESDEMONA KILLINGSWORTH (40s, clipboard, cheerful menace), rattles keys. And behind the register, a nervous newcomer, TIBERIUS "TIBBY" MOREAU-WELLS (teen, first shift, sweating through his shirt), tries to look busy.

BARN
Juniper, where's the iguana?

JUNIPER
In his tank.

BARN
Which tank?

JUNIPER
The big one.

BARN
That one's empty.

JUNIPER
(slowly)
Oh.

They look at each other. Both turn toward Mr. Pruitt-Hale, who is still peering into the empty tank.

EZEKIEL
Fascinating. Where's the lizard?

TIBBY
(very high)
He's resting!

DESDEMONA
Tibby, don't lie to the customer.

TIBBY
I'm not lying! I'm hopeful!

EZEKIEL
Young man, I've bought four geckos from this store. I know when a tank is vacant.

BARN
Sir, it's a rare species.

EZEKIEL
Camouflaged?

JUNIPER
Extremely.

A thump from the ceiling. All five look up. A long green tail hangs from a ceiling tile, swaying gently. Tibby faints into the cricket display. Desdemona sighs and heads for a ladder.
